  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Rigor + load">Emit RIGOR LEVEL (FULL). Load knowledge files. Read task 084's `Invoke-CatalogGenerator.ps1` end-to-end to understand `Test-ManifestShape` + emitters + `-Verify` mechanism. Read existing `Configure-AppServiceSettings.generated.ps1` to see current batched-write shape.</step>
    <step order="1" name="BFF IOptions inventory">Enumerate every `AddOptions&lt;T&gt;().Bind(...).ValidateOnStart()` chain in `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/DI/*Module.cs`. Produce table: module name, IOptions type, config section, required fields per validator. Cross-check against `docs/guides/SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md` § App Service settings. Identify which fields resolve via KV-refs (already in existing `secrets:` list) vs need per-env-literal (subject of this task).</step>
    <step order="2" name="Manifest schema extension">Extend `scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/manifest.yaml` with NEW top-level list `per_env_settings:`. Populate with entries per BFF IOptions inventory (Step 1). Schema per entry: key, per_env_source, literal_value?, iOptionsModule, required, notes?. Coordinate with task 200 landing: 200's `from-shared-service` addition to `value_source` enum must land FIRST (both tasks touch same file; sequential land avoids merge headache).</step>
    <step order="3" name="Generator extension">Extend `Invoke-CatalogGenerator.ps1`: (a) add `$RequiredPerEnvSettingFields` array + `Test-PerEnvSettingsShape` function mirroring existing `Test-ManifestShape` pattern; (b) extend `Write-ConfigureAppServiceSettings` (or equivalent function) to emit per-env-literal lines into `Configure-AppServiceSettings.generated.ps1` after existing KV-ref lines (both in same `$settings = @(...)` array so batched call preserved); (c) add script parameters to generated Configure script signature for each unique per-env source; (d) sort per_env_settings entries alphabetically by `key` for determinism.</step>
    <step order="4" name="Regenerate + verify">Run `pwsh Invoke-CatalogGenerator.ps1 -DryRun` — inspect planned Configure script diff. Run write mode; commit regenerated `Configure-AppServiceSettings.generated.ps1`. Run `-Verify` — exit 0 required.</step>
    <step order="5" name="IProcessRunner + PwshProcessRunner">Check if `IProcessRunner` exists elsewhere in `.Core` (e.g., in `IntegrationWiring` for H14a); REUSE if so. Otherwise author: `Task&lt;ProcessResult&gt; RunAsync(string executable, string[] args, IReadOnlyDictionary&lt;string,string&gt; env, CancellationToken ct)`. `ProcessResult` is a record: `{ ExitCode, Stdout, Stderr }`. Impl uses `System.Diagnostics.Process` with async stdio capture + timeout.</step>
    <step order="6" name="IHealthzProbe + HttpHealthzProbe">Author interface: `Task&lt;HealthzResult&gt; ProbeWithBackoffAsync(string healthzUrl, CancellationToken ct)`. Backoff schedule 30s/60s/90s/120s/180s. `HealthzResult` = `Success` or `Timeout`. Impl uses `HttpClient` (injected). Total budget 8 min.</step>
    <step order="7" name="IContainerLogFetcher + KuduContainerLogFetcher">Author interface: `Task&lt;string&gt; FetchDockerLogsAsync(string appServiceName, TokenCredential credential, CancellationToken ct)`. Impl fetches from Kudu API `/api/logs/docker` (with bearer token from credential); also fetches most recent log file via `/api/vfs/LogFiles/*_docker.log` if primary is empty. Parses for `Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException:` line + extracts module name via regex.</step>
    <step order="8" name="H4bBulkAppSettingsHandler impl">Author main handler mirroring H4 shape. Ctor injects: IProvisioningRunRepository, IProcessRunner, IHealthzProbe, IContainerLogFetcher, IOptions&lt;BulkAppSettingsOptions&gt;, IManifestReader (for per_env_settings list — probably extend existing IKvSecretManifest OR add sibling IPerEnvSettingsManifest — decide during Step 4 based on IKvSecretManifest surface), ILogger. HandleAsync flow: (a) load per_env_settings from manifest; (b) resolve values from envelope.Parameters.NonSecret — fail early on missing required; (c) build `pwsh` args from resolved values; (d) invoke IProcessRunner; if exit non-zero → Failure(Resumable, "AppSettingsWriteFailed", stdout+stderr); (e) invoke IHealthzProbe; if Timeout → invoke IContainerLogFetcher + parse → Failure(QuarantineRequired, "HealthzTimeout", parsed diagnostic); (f) return Success(idempotencyKey `appsettings-{env}-{manifestVer}`). HandlerId const: `HandlerIds.H4b`.</step>
    <step order="9" name="DI 3-file registration">(a) `.Core\Handlers\HandlerIds.cs`: add `public const string H4b = "h4b";` near H4 const; append to `Dispatchable` list. (b) `.Core\Handlers\HandlerDispatchRegistrationModule.cs`: add keyed forwarder for `HandlerIds.H4b`. (c) `.Worker\Program.cs`: add concrete registrations near H4 block: Options bind, IProcessRunner + IHealthzProbe + IContainerLogFetcher AddSingleton, H4b AddScoped.</step>
    <step order="10" name="Unit tests">Author `H4bBulkAppSettingsHandlerTests.cs` in `.Tests\Handlers\`. xunit + FluentAssertions + hand-rolled fakes. Tests: (a) full-manifest happy path — mock IProcessRunner returns exit 0, mock IHealthzProbe returns Success → HandlerResult.Success; (b) per-env-input missing → Failure(Resumable, "PerEnvInputMissing", diagnostic) BEFORE process invocation; (c) PS non-zero exit → Failure(Resumable, "AppSettingsWriteFailed", stdout+stderr); (d) /healthz timeout with parsed module name → Failure(QuarantineRequired, "HealthzTimeout", "BFF fail-fast on {moduleName}: ..."); (e) /healthz timeout with unparseable log → Failure(QuarantineRequired, "HealthzTimeout", generic diagnostic); (f) BINDING guard — synthetic manifest with `Dataverse-ClientSecret` in `per_env_settings` → generator `Test-ManifestShape` fails validation (this is a generator test, not a handler test — belongs in a PS test if `Pester` in use, else document as manual verification); (g) idempotency-key match — same (env, manifestVer) → short-circuit via IIdempotencyService pattern (mirror H4).</step>
    <step order="11" name="Build + test">Run `dotnet build src/server/services/Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Core/` + `dotnet build src/server/services/Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Worker/` + `dotnet test src/server/services/Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Tests/`. All exit 0. `HandlerRegistrationCompletenessTests` must pass.</step>
    <step order="12" name="Regenerated-outputs Verify">Run `pwsh scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/Invoke-CatalogGenerator.ps1 -Verify` — exit 0 required.</step>
    <step order="13" name="Handler-catalog update (main-session)">Update `.claude/skills/provision-environment/SKILL.md` Step 3 enumeration: H4b row already listed there from SESSION 3; verify accuracy vs implemented handler (main-session-only per root CLAUDE.md §3).</step>
    <step order="14" name="TASK-INDEX update (main-session)">Update TASK-INDEX.md: task 201 status 🔲 → ✅ with commit hash.</step>
    <step order="15" name="Deviations">Document any deviation in projects/customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1/notes/task-201-completion-notes.md.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If task 200 (H4-shared) has NOT landed, STOP and escalate — H4b's KV-ref entries (emitted by existing generator from `secrets:` list) resolve to secrets H4-shared seeds. Running H4b without H4-shared produces `KeyVaultReferenceCouldNotResolve` at BFF runtime.</trigger>
    <trigger>If BFF IOptions inventory (Step 1) reveals &gt;60 modules (significantly more than the ~40 expected), STOP and escalate — signals ADR-010 DI-minimalism drift + should trigger a decomposition project BEFORE H4b lands. Manifest scope explodes proportionally.</trigger>
    <trigger>If a required per-env-input is not yet emitted by any upstream handler (e.g., `WebhookSigningKey` missing from H8's interStepState), STOP and escalate — H4b cannot fabricate the value; the upstream handler must be extended first.</trigger>
    <trigger>If /healthz-never-green diagnostic parses a fail-fast module name NOT present in the manifest, STOP and add manifest entry → regenerate → re-run. If the parse also names a module NOT present in the BFF IOptions inventory (Step 1), the inventory is stale — re-run Step 1.</trigger>
    <trigger>If `-Verify` fails after manifest + generator extensions, STOP and diagnose: (a) generator emitter added output but ordering non-deterministic; (b) task 200's `from-shared-service` extension collides with task 201's `per_env_settings` extension; (c) determinism contract violated.</trigger>
    <trigger>If `HandlerRegistrationCompletenessTests` fails after DI 3-file edits, one of the three files is missing an update.</trigger>
    <trigger>If task 200 and task 201 both need to modify `manifest.yaml` in the same PR: land task 200 FIRST (smaller extension: enum value + optional field); then task 201's PR extends with new top-level list. Avoids merge conflict on the same file.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>Closest neighbors: (a) H4 per-tenant handler (task 047, landed) — writes to per-tenant KV, does NOT set App Service app settings. (b) Task 084's shipped `Configure-AppServiceSettings.generated.ps1` — batches app-settings writes but emits KV-refs ONLY (SESSION 4 recon confirmed). (c) `az webapp config appsettings set --settings` ad-hoc — SESSION 2 used this manually for ONE setting (`SpeAdmin__KeyVaultUri`), revealed F20a next fail-fast. (d) Bicep `app-service.bicep` `appSettings` array — sets initial app settings at deploy time but stale across upgrades + cannot resolve per-env inputs from run state. Grep for `BulkAppSettings` OR `HealthzProbe` OR `PerEnvSettingsManifest` returns none.</existing>
    <extension>The existing Configure-AppServiceSettings.generated.ps1 can be EXTENDED (Option A per SESSION 4 discussion): add `per_env_settings:` top-level list to manifest; generator emits per-env-literal lines into the SAME Configure script alongside existing KV-ref lines. This is the RIGHT extension because it preserves task 084's single source of truth. The H4b HANDLER cannot be extension of H4 per-tenant because (a) different write target (App Service settings vs KV secrets), (b) different orchestration (batched write + restart + /healthz poll + log parse vs per-secret write), (c) different failure mode (HealthzTimeout requires parsing docker logs — H4 has no analog).</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Without H4b as a handler: (1) Every new BFF customer stamp requires MANUAL enumeration of ~40 IOptions modules → ~80-160 app settings → either author them one-by-one per stamp (2 hours manual work per customer) OR run the shipped Configure script from a workstation (works, but no automation trail in Cosmos run state + no /healthz post-condition check + no diagnostic parsing on failure). (2) Every BFF module addition (new IOptions binding) silently breaks new customer stand-ups until an operator manually adds the app setting — no drift detection today; Option A extension gives us drift detection via `-Verify`. (3) F20's fail-fast pattern (SIGABRT exit 134) will recur on EVERY new Model 1 shared-tier deploy AND on EVERY Model 2 Dedicated deploy without H4b — this is not a Model-1-specific bug; it's the fresh-deploy-without-bulk-config-and-healthz-probe bug. Concrete observed failure mode SESSION 2: `Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException: SpeAdmin:KeyVaultUri (or KeyVaultUri) configuration is required for SpeAdminModule.` at BFF container startup, followed by SIGABRT (exit 134), followed by App Service Failed-to-start. Without automated parsing of docker logs to extract the module name, operator triaging takes 15-30 min per fail; H4b makes it 30 seconds.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

